WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.980
Proposition thirty-eight asks California to borrow eight point four billion

2
00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:10.760
dollars for immunology and immunotherapy research. Immunology studies the

3
00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:15.840
immune system; immunotherapy aims to use it to fight disease. This funds

4
00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:21.260
research, not a guaranteed new treatment. Universities, businesses, and

5
00:00:21.260 --> 00:00:25.760
governments already fund medical research. Many projects never become

6
00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:30.460
approved treatments. A research bond supplies money now, while the state

7
00:00:30.460 --> 00:00:33.200
repays the borrowing with interest over time.

8
00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:38.640
Imagine a scientist studying immune cells. A promising laboratory result

9
00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:43.780
still needs testing for safety and effectiveness. Some ideas fail, and

10
00:00:43.780 --> 00:00:48.100
successful ones can take years. Funding more work can create opportunities

11
00:00:48.100 --> 00:00:53.140
without letting anyone honestly promise when a patient will benefit.

12
00:00:53.140 --> 00:00:56.720
Research teams need facilities, people, and sustained funding.

13
00:00:57.233 --> 00:01:01.333
About half the money would go to one qualifying research institute selected

14
00:01:01.333 --> 00:01:05.553
by the state. The other half would support competitive grants through a

15
00:01:05.553 --> 00:01:10.053
council of research institutions. Reporting and financial audits are

16
00:01:10.053 --> 00:01:15.053
required. At least four point two billion dollars must support research on

17
00:01:15.053 --> 00:01:20.273
cancer, heart disease, and Alzheimer’s disease. Treatments developed with

18
00:01:20.273 --> 00:01:23.833
the funding generally must be offered at a twenty percent discount in

19
00:01:23.833 --> 00:01:29.213
California. A discount matters only if a treatment reaches patients. The

20
00:01:29.213 --> 00:01:33.233
analyst estimates five hundred to six hundred million dollars a year in

21
00:01:33.233 --> 00:01:37.633
General Fund repayments for about twenty years. Revenue from discoveries

22
00:01:37.633 --> 00:01:42.593
could offset costs, but its amount and timing are uncertain and could take

23
00:01:42.593 --> 00:01:43.213
decades.

24
00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:48.240
A voter might see long-term public research as worth supporting even when

25
00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:53.280
returns are uncertain. Discoveries could improve lives and build scientific

26
00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:58.980
capacity. They may accept borrowing because waiting for annual funding also

27
00:01:58.980 --> 00:02:00.420
has an opportunity cost.

28
00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:05.240
You might ask whether this is the best research investment. Another voter

29
00:02:05.240 --> 00:02:10.300
could object to the debt, the concentration of funding in one institute, or

30
00:02:10.300 --> 00:02:14.380
committing money before knowing results. They might favor broader

31
00:02:14.380 --> 00:02:19.500
competition or other health priorities. The possibility of royalties should

32
00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:22.940
not be treated as a promise that the bond pays for itself.

33
00:02:23.533 --> 00:02:29.233
In the September PPIC survey of likely voters, 55 percent said yes, 42

34
00:02:29.233 --> 00:02:35.153
percent no, and 3 percent were unsure. These are dated survey results, not

35
00:02:35.153 --> 00:02:36.273
votes already cast.

36
00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:42.040
Yes authorizes this research borrowing; no rejects this bond without ending

37
00:02:42.040 --> 00:02:47.260
existing medical research. Visit our website for the fuller explanation,

38
00:02:47.260 --> 00:02:51.780
source links, and official voter guide. Compare the tradeoffs, then make

39
00:02:51.780 --> 00:02:53.440
your own informed choice.
